    "description": "Social justice in Iran involves equality in everyday opportunities, protection against discrimination, and access to effective remedies when rights are violated. Research on gender, minority rights, disability, and accountability reveals distinct barriers that cannot be understood through household income alone.",
    "tags": ["Social justice", "Equality", "Human rights", "Discrimination", "Accountability"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:39:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:39:22Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Social Justice in Iran: Equality, Rights and Accountability",
      "description": "Explore documented barriers to equality in Iran, including gender discrimination, minority rights, disability inclusion and access to justice."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "understanding-justice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Equality, opportunity and remedy",
        "content": "Social justice offers a way to connect questions that otherwise appear separate: who can pursue education or employment, whose circumstances public institutions accommodate, and whether people can challenge unfair treatment. In Iran, these questions involve both discriminatory rules and barriers encountered when seeking protection. Human Rights Watch’s account of events in 2024 identifies gender discrimination, restrictions affecting minorities, and serious shortcomings in judicial proceedings. These are documented findings about particular institutions and practices, rather than a description of every interaction within Iranian society. [s1]\n\nIt is useful to distinguish equal treatment from effective inclusion. An identical procedure can still exclude someone who needs accessible materials, physical assistance or another reasonable accommodation. UNICEF’s work on disability-inclusive education in Iran identifies shortcomings in accessible infrastructure and learning tools, alongside limitations in disability identification. This makes the design of ordinary public services part of the social-justice question. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "gender-minority-rights",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Gender and minority rights",
        "content": "Human Rights Watch’s 2025 country chapter describes unequal rules affecting women in marriage, divorce, inheritance and decisions concerning children. It also documents restrictions on cultural and political activities among ethnic minorities and discrimination against religious minorities. These findings need to be read with their reporting period attached; they should not substitute for checking the legal text and its implementation at a later date. [s1]\n\nThe UN Independent International Fact-Finding Mission investigates violations through a different mandate from a domestic court. Presenting its findings in March 2025, the Mission connected structural discrimination against women with violations surrounding the Woman, Life, Freedom protests that began in September 2022. It also reported particular targeting of ethnic and religious minorities. Attribution matters: these are findings of an international investigative mechanism, rather than judgments establishing the criminal liability of each individual involved. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "accountability",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why accountability matters",
        "content": "A remedy involves more than the existence of a complaint procedure. The Fact-Finding Mission’s March 2025 presentation described persistent impunity and harassment of victims and families seeking accountability. Its recommendations addressed truth, justice and reparations, making clear that documenting harm and repairing its consequences are related but separate tasks. [s2]\n\nThe Abdorrahman Boroumand Center’s library summary of the Mission’s September 2026 report describes further findings concerning suppression of protests beginning in December 2025. It also records major investigative limitations, including denied access to Iran, communications restrictions and fear of reprisals. This summary provides a recent pointer, but the underlying report requires direct inspection before its detailed findings can be fully incorporated here. [s4]\n\nReading social justice through these distinctions avoids treating either a formal entitlement or a reported reform as proof of an effective remedy. The central editorial question remains whether people can exercise their rights, challenge violations safely and obtain meaningful redress. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      }
    ],
