    "description": "Social vulnerability concerns exposure to harm and the resources available to prevent, withstand or recover from it. In Iran, research on child protection, disability, social assistance and emergencies shows how household circumstances and institutional barriers can overlap.",
    "tags": ["Social vulnerability", "Child protection", "Disability inclusion", "Social protection", "Emergency response"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:39:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:39:22Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Social Vulnerabilities in Iran: Protection, Inclusion and Resilience",
      "description": "Understand overlapping risks affecting children and families in Iran, from violence and disability barriers to gaps in social protection and emergency support."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "overlapping-risks",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Vulnerability is shaped by circumstances",
        "content": "Social vulnerability should describe a relationship between exposure to harm and available protection, rather than become a permanent label attached to a person. In Iran, the evidence points to overlapping difficulties: violence can affect a child’s health and education, while inaccessible services can increase the burden on a family already providing care. UNICEF identifies children with disabilities, children in street situations and children in alternative care as groups facing particular protection risks. These categories describe different circumstances and should not be treated as interchangeable. [s1]\n\nThe same distinction applies to the institutions intended to help. Cash assistance, social work, medical treatment and accessible education address different needs. A household’s receipt of one form of support does not establish that the others are available. A policy analysis published in 2022 accordingly called for closer links between cash transfers and education, health and protection services. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "child-protection",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Violence, reporting and response",
        "content": "UNICEF’s Iran programme describes violence against children as including physical, emotional and sexual harm, neglect and emerging online abuse. Its account identifies data scarcity, weak coordination and referral mechanisms, inadequate prevention services, stigma and social norms as obstacles to prevention and reporting. These limitations matter when interpreting case totals: a service’s recorded caseload cannot be assumed to capture every child experiencing harm. [s1]\n\nThe programme’s proposed response includes workforce training, parenting support, counselling and community engagement. These are descriptions of programme activities and objectives, rather than independent evidence that violence has declined. Assessing results requires information about whether children can reach services safely, whether referrals lead to assistance and whether protection continues after an initial contact. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "social-protection",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Assistance and the limits of programme coverage",
        "content": "The International Policy Centre for Inclusive Growth’s 2022 analysis describes a social-protection landscape involving the Ministry of Cooperatives, Labour and Social Welfare, the State Welfare Organization and the Imam Khomeini Relief Foundation. It identifies the Iranian Welfare Database as an important tool for beneficiary identification, while arguing that information needs updating to improve responsiveness to shocks. [s2]\n\nThe same analysis highlights access for people in remote areas and Afghan refugees, extension of contributory protection to informal workers, and weaknesses in monitoring and evaluation. These are dated policy findings, not a verified description of every programme operating in 2026. They nevertheless explain why counting recipients alone cannot establish whether assistance reaches those facing the greatest barriers. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "emergencies",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Emergencies can interrupt existing support",
        "content": "UNICEF’s August 2026 account describes conflict-related disruption to health care, water, sanitation, protection and psychosocial support. Its response included community-based counselling and assistance for children without effective caregivers. Such reporting shows the importance of maintaining care relationships alongside repairing physical infrastructure. It does not provide a national estimate of unmet protection needs or demonstrate that recovery was complete by the editorial cutoff. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
